<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_skip.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-skip">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-skip">
  <primary>SKIP</primary>
 </indexterm>
 <indexterm zone="cypher-skip">
  <primary>OFFSET</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>SKIP</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>SKIP</refname>
  <refpurpose>discard working rows from the front of the result</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
{ SKIP | OFFSET } <replaceable class="parameter">count</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-skip-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>SKIP</command> discards the first <replaceable class="parameter">count</replaceable>
   rows and passes on the rest. It is a clause of its own, so it can page the rows anywhere
   in a query. Write it after a <xref linkend="cypher-return"/> or a
   <xref linkend="cypher-with"/>, or straight after a <xref linkend="cypher-match"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>SKIP</command> and <command>OFFSET</command> are one clause with two spellings.
   They parse to the same node and behave identically, so use whichever reads better.
   <command>OFFSET</command> is the spelling ISO/IEC 39075 lists first.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>LIMIT</command> caps how many rows are kept instead. It is a separate clause with
   a page of its own, and either clause may be used without the other. See
   <xref linkend="cypher-limit"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The count is an expression, not only a literal, and it is evaluated once. A negative count
   is an error. A count larger than the number of rows is not: the query returns no rows. A
   fractional count is rounded to the nearest integer.
  </para>

  <para>
   Without an <xref linkend="cypher-orderby"/>, which rows are discarded is not defined. Sort
   first whenever the page has to be reproducible.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>SKIP</command> cannot open a query and cannot end one. A statement that starts
   with it is a syntax error, and a query that stops on it raises
   <literal>Cypher query must end with RETURN, FINISH or update clause</literal>. Finish with
   <command>RETURN</command>, <command>FINISH</command> or a write clause.
  </para>

  <para>
   Written after a projection, the modifiers have to be in the order
   <command>ORDER BY</command>, then <command>SKIP</command> or <command>OFFSET</command>,
   then <command>LIMIT</command>. Only that order folds into the projection. In the middle of
   a query both orders parse and they mean different things, because each clause applies to
   the rows the clause before it produced. See <xref linkend="cypher-skip-notes"/>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-skip-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">count</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      An integer expression that is not negative. That many leading rows are discarded. A
      fraction is rounded to the nearest integer.
     </para>
     <para>
      A negative count is refused with <literal>OFFSET must not be negative</literal>, under
      that name whichever spelling of the clause was written, because the two are one clause.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-skip-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Take a page from the middle of a sorted result:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
RETURN m.title AS film
ORDER BY film
SKIP 2
LIMIT 3;

        film
---------------------
 "Blade Runner 2049"
 "Elysium"
 "Gattaca"
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>OFFSET</command> reads the same:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
RETURN m.title AS film
ORDER BY film
OFFSET 2
LIMIT 3;

        film
---------------------
 "Blade Runner 2049"
 "Elysium"
 "Gattaca"
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Any expression will do as the count:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
RETURN m.title AS film
ORDER BY film
SKIP 1+1
LIMIT 2;

        film
---------------------
 "Blade Runner 2049"
 "Elysium"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Paging past the end is not an error. There are eighteen films, so nothing is left:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
RETURN m.title AS film
ORDER BY film
SKIP 20;

 film
------
(0 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A negative count is refused:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) RETURN m.title AS film ORDER BY film SKIP -1;

ERROR:  OFFSET must not be negative
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-skip-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   After a projection, write the modifiers in the order <command>ORDER BY</command>, then
   <command>SKIP</command> or <command>OFFSET</command>, then <command>LIMIT</command>. Only
   that order folds into the projection. Put <command>LIMIT</command> first and the trailing
   <command>SKIP</command> is read as a clause of its own, so the query no longer ends on a
   projection. That is what the error reports:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) RETURN m.title AS film ORDER BY film LIMIT 3 SKIP 2;

ERROR:  Cypher query must end with RETURN, FINISH or update clause
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   In the middle of a query both orders parse and they answer differently. Each clause applies
   to the rows the clause before it produced. Skipping first takes rows three to six:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) ORDER BY m.title SKIP 2 LIMIT 4 RETURN m.title AS film;

        film
---------------------
 "Blade Runner 2049"
 "Elysium"
 "Gattaca"
 "Inception"
(4 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Limiting first cuts to four rows and then drops two of them, leaving two:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) ORDER BY m.title LIMIT 4 SKIP 2 RETURN m.title AS film;

        film
---------------------
 "Blade Runner 2049"
 "Elysium"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>skip</literal> is an unreserved keyword, so it stays available as a variable, a
   label or a property name. <literal>offset</literal> is reserved and has to be quoted to be
   used as a name.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-skip-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>SKIP</command> implements the offset clause of ISO/IEC 39075:2024, section 16.19,
   as part of the order by and page statement of section 14.9. The offset synonym the standard
   defines is <literal>OFFSET | SKIP</literal>, so both spellings conform. The standard's count
   is an integer that is not negative, and AgensGraph accepts any integer expression in that
   position, rounding a fraction.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher has only <command>SKIP</command>, and writes it as a trailing modifier of
   <command>RETURN</command> or <command>WITH</command>. Both of those forms are accepted here
   unchanged. The standalone clause, which pages the rows after a <command>MATCH</command> or
   anywhere else in a query, is the addition.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

